#
# Where a `$( … )` body refused inside an expansion's operand is located —
# #3810.
#
# An operand is read again when the word is expanded, so the spans it yields
# were numbered from the operand's own first line and not from the file's.
# That number reaches a reader: the body below was located at **line 1**
# whatever line held it, in every dialect, where every reference shell names
# the line it is on.
#
# bash writes the line twice for this failure — once in the prefix and once
# in front of the script line it echoes underneath — so one case grades the
# placement and the echo together. A copy of the shape in
# ksh/substitution-line.tests rather than a shared file, because the wording
# is this column's.
#
# The controls come first: `$LINENO` says the file's own numbering is what it
# looks like, and a body that parses inside an operand says the operand is
# expanded at all. Neither can be written as a second *failure*, because this
# one ends the script wherever it stands — a subshell included.
#
# One failing case, and it is last: the failure ends the script.

printf 'the controls first\n'
printf 'this line -> [%s]\n' "$LINENO"
printf 'an ordinary body -> [%s]\n' "${u:-$(echo hi)}"

printf 'the case\n'
echo ${u:-$(echo hi
for)}
printf 'never reached\n'
